<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">When a tool-calling agent picks the wrong tool, the failure is invisible until execution: the email gets sent, the meeting gets missed. Probing 12 instruction-tuned models across Gemma 3, Qwen 3, Qwen 2.5, and Llama 3.1 (270M to 27B), we find the identity of the chosen tool is linearly readable and steerable inside the model. Adding the mean-difference between two tools’ average internal activations switches which tool the model selects at 77–100% accuracy on name-only single-turn prompts (93–100% at 4B+), and the JSON arguments that follow autoregressively match the new tool’s schema, so flipping the name is enough. The same per-tool means also flag likely errors before they happen: on Gemma 3 12B and 27B, queries where the gap between the top-1 and top-2 tool is smallest produce 14–21<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> more wrong calls than queries with the largest gap. The causal effect concentrates along one direction, the row of the output layer that produces the target tool’s first token: a unit vector along it at matched magnitude already reaches 93–100%, while what is left over leaves the choice almost untouched. Activation patching localises this to a small set of mid- and late-layer attention heads, and a within-topic probe across 14 same-domain <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-bench airline tools reaches top-1 61–89% across five 4B–14B models, ruling out the reading that we are just moving the model along a topic axis. Even base models encode the right tool before they can emit it: cosine readout from the internal state recovers 69–82% on BFCL while base generation reaches only 2–10%, suggesting pretraining forms the representation and instruction tuning later wires it to the output. We measure tool identity <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">selection</em> and JSON schema correctness in single-turn fixed-menu settings; multi-turn agentic transfer is more fragile and is discussed in Limitations.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07990v1/figures/fig1_hero_final.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/192;" width="476" height="192" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of the three-stage circuit and steering demonstration. Adding a mean-difference vector redirects tool selection and automatically restructures arguments. Validated across 12 IT models in 3 families (Gemma 3, Qwen 3 / Qwen 2.5, Llama 3.1; 270M–27B).</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Imagine an LLM assistant is asked to “follow up with the client about tomorrow’s meeting.” It has access to <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">send_email</span>, <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">schedule_meeting</span>, and <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">search_contacts</span>. The model picks <span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">send_email</span>, writes a plausible message, and fires it off. But the user wanted to <em id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reschedule</em>, not send a reminder. The email goes out; the meeting is missed. This kind of silent failure is common: on the <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-bench airline benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, even 4B-parameter models only succeed 25% of the time, and most failures come down to picking the wrong tool <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schick et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. As agents get access to actions that matter (running code, moving money, approving transactions), one bad tool call can do real damage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The tricky part is that we currently have no idea <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">why</em> a model picks one tool over another. It outputs a tool name and some JSON arguments, but there is no way to peek inside and catch a mistake before it happens. Recent work has started looking at this: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Healy et al. (2026)</a></cite> detect tool-calling hallucinations from hidden states, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2026)</a></cite> improve binary decisions about whether to call a tool at all (F1: 0.18<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>0.50). But neither of these traces the actual circuit responsible for selection, and neither shows how to control <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which specific tool</em> gets chosen when there are many candidates. The question we ask is simple: <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">how do language models internally pick among tools, and can we intervene on that process?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate this by combining five interpretability methods across three model families (Gemma 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemma Team, 2024</a>)</cite>, Qwen 3 / Qwen 2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen Team, 2025</a>)</cite>, and Llama 3.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>), spanning 270M to 27B parameters across 12 instruction-tuned models. Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> gives the overview.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The core finding is that tool identity is linearly readable and intervenable inside the model. The model’s internal state (called the residual stream) has 2,560 dimensions, and a single mean-difference vector along it suffices to switch which tool the model selects. PCA over 15 tool means fits in <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>10 directions, but this number is not by itself evidence of a tool-specific subspace: a matched-prompt control where we keep the 15-tool prefix and only change the user query gives equally compact spectra (<math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}{=}7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}{=}7</annotation></semantics></math>–9 across four models). The picture at much larger tool counts is more nuanced: 36 directions cover 200 real APIs from ToolBench, but a matched-prompt control where we keep the same 200-tool prompt prefix and only change the user query gives <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}{=}39" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>39</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}{=}39</annotation></semantics></math>, so the K=200 number partly reflects shared prompt structure rather than tool-specific compression (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Tool identity fits in a small space ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). This holds across all 12 instruction-tuned models and the Gemma and Llama base variants we tested (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Tool identity fits in a small space ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). Because the structure is so simple, two things become possible. First, we can switch which tool the model picks by adding a direction vector (the average internal state for the target tool minus the source tool). Using only tool names, this achieves 77–100% across 12 instruction-tuned models (4B+ reaches 93–100%). Adding the kind of short descriptions that real function-calling systems include offers at most a small boost for the 4B+ models (still 83–100%), while it hurts some sub-1B models (Appendix <a href="#A23" title="Appendix A.23 Does adding tool descriptions change the results? ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.23</span></a>); once the tool name is committed, the JSON arguments autoregressively follow the new tool’s schema (Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 The model also rewrites its arguments ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>). Second, we can tell when the model is about to make a mistake: if the top two tools are nearly tied internally, it is much more likely to pick the wrong one.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Base (pretrained-only) models extend this picture. On BFCL v3, a Gemma 3 4B base model generates the correct tool on only about 3% of queries, but reading the closest mean-activation direction from its residual stream recovers 75%; the gap is similar on Gemma 1B, 12B, and Llama 3.1 8B base models (56–72 percentage points). Instruction tuning, it seems, mostly wires the existing internal tool signal into the output layer, rather than creating the signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To understand <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> this works, we trace the components that push the model toward the right tool (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Tracing the decision from input to output ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). Patching components in and out one at a time, we find a rough three-stage pathway: dedicated features light up at early layers when a specific tool is relevant, attention heads at mid-layers pull in the right context, and late layers make the final pick. A few specific attention heads carry the decision on Gemma (like L17 H0 and H1 in Gemma 3 4B), but the picture is model-dependent: on Qwen 3 4B the top-<math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> heads and top-<math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> MLPs are essentially tied (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Tracing the decision from input to output ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, Table <a href="#A24.T26" title="Table 26 ‣ Appendix A.24 How we test which components matter (activation patching) ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>). The 80–88% raw attention share is partly an artefact of these models having many more attention heads than MLPs. The circuit is not always present: it is absent at 270M parameters, starts emerging at 1B, and gets sharper with instruction tuning (Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 When does this structure appear? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>). For practitioners, this means representation-based monitoring only works at 1B and above.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Most work on tool use in LLMs focuses on making models better at calling tools, whether through specialized training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schick et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patil et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>, or prompt design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. We are asking a different question: not how to improve tool use, but how it works internally. Two recent papers look in this direction. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Healy et al. (2026)</a></cite> show that tool-calling hallucinations leave a detectable trace in hidden states, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2026)</a></cite> steer binary tool/no-tool decisions (F1: 0.18<math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>0.50). We go further, from binary to multi-class: steering among 15+ tools with structured output adaptation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our work builds on the idea that language models represent high-level concepts as directions in activation space, sometimes called the linear representation hypothesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nanda et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. This has been confirmed for things like sentiment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tigges et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, truthfulness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marks and Tegmark, 2024</a>)</cite>, and refusal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arditi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, though not everything is linear <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Engels et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Several methods exploit this linearity to steer model behavior at inference time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turner et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The closest prior work to ours is by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Todd et al. (2024)</a></cite>, who find that in-context learning tasks are encoded as “function vectors.” The key difference is that their vectors capture <em id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">task type</em> (like translation or capitalization), while ours capture <em id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which specific tool to call from a fixed menu</em>, and our steering produces structured JSON output, not just a label change.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On the mechanistic side, we combine three tools to trace how the model makes its decision. Activation patching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vig et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conmy et al., 2023</a>)</cite> tests which components matter. Sparse autoencoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cunningham et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bricken et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Templeton et al., 2024</a>)</cite> identify individual features. Cross-layer transcoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ameisen et al., 2025</a>)</cite> decompose computation layer by layer. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geva et al. (2023)</a></cite> found that factual recall follows an “attribute then retrieve” pattern, and we see a loosely similar shape for tool selection. The superposition framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elhage et al., 2022</a>)</cite> also predicts that discrete categories sit in nearly orthogonal directions, which fits with our <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>10-dimensional subspace for 15 tools.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method: Mean-Difference Steering for Tool Selection</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The idea is straightforward. If different tools produce different average activation patterns, then the difference between two tool averages gives us a direction in activation space that points from one tool to another. Adding that direction during generation should switch the model’s tool choice. No training, no gradients, no SAEs required; just a few example queries per tool and one forward pass to collect activations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We test this across three model families: Gemma 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemma Team, 2024</a>)</cite> (270M, 1B, 4B, 12B, 27B); Qwen 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen Team, 2025</a>)</cite> (0.6B, 1.7B, 4B, 8B, 14B) plus Qwen 2.5 (7B); and Llama 3.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite> (8B), totaling 12 instruction-tuned models plus base (pre-trained) variants for Gemma and Llama. Circuit tracing with transcoders uses Gemma 3 4B (34 layers, <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{model}}=2560" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2560</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{model}}=2560</annotation></semantics></math>) as the reference. Each prompt lists <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> tool definitions followed by a user query. Mechanism experiments (steering, circuit tracing, PCA at <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}15</annotation></semantics></math>) use controlled synthetic tools where we can isolate individual variables like tool naming and query semantics. Scaling and validation experiments use real APIs from <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> (14–16 tools), ToolBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> (up to 2,000 APIs from 49 domains), and BFCL v3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> (1,053 real-world queries). We also apply SAEs, activation patching, and PCA to understand the internal representations, but the steering itself needs none of that machinery.
The method has three steps. First, for each tool <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we collect <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2\text{--}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2\text{--}3</annotation></semantics></math> queries that should trigger that tool and record the model’s internal state (its “activation”) <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{i}^{(j)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{i}^{(j)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> at the final token position in the second-to-last layer <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=33" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>33</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=33</annotation></semantics></math> for Gemma 3 4B, <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>35</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=35</annotation></semantics></math> for Qwen 3 4B). The mean activation per tool is <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{i}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j}\mathbf{h}_{i}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{i}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j}\mathbf{h}_{i}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Second, the steering direction from tool <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> to tool <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is just the normalized mean difference:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{a\to b}=\frac{\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{b}-\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{a}}{\|\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{b}-\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{a}\|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{a\to b}=\frac{\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{b}-\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{a}}{\|\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{b}-\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{a}\|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We scale it by <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\cdot\text{sep}_{a\to b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext>sep</mtext><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\cdot\text{sep}_{a\to b}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sep}_{a\to b}=(\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{b}-\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{a})\cdot\mathbf{d}_{a\to b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>sep</mtext><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sep}_{a\to b}=(\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{b}-\bar{\mathbf{h}}_{a})\cdot\mathbf{d}_{a\to b}</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection gap between the two tool means. We pick <math id="S3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0.5,0.7,1.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0.5,0.7,1.0\}</annotation></semantics></math> using a 5-pair validation set disjoint from the test pairs.
Third, during generation we add this vector to the residual stream at layer <math id="S3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{\prime}_{\ell}=\mathbf{h}_{\ell}+\alpha\cdot\text{sep}_{a\to b}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{a\to b}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext>sep</mtext><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{\prime}_{\ell}=\mathbf{h}_{\ell}+\alpha\cdot\text{sep}_{a\to b}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{a\to b}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">We only intervene at the final token position on the first forward pass. No weights change; subsequent tokens are generated normally. This extends activation addition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turner et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arditi et al., 2024</a>)</cite> from binary features to multi-class tool selection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We count a steer as successful if the model’s next-token prediction matches the target tool’s name prefix. Because four tools share the prefix <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">get_</span> (weather, stock_price, news, directions), a single-token check cannot distinguish them; we report both prefix-match and exact-match (via 5-token generation) rates and note the difference explicitly. All accuracy claims are tested against random chance (<math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/K</annotation></semantics></math>), with 95% confidence intervals. We use standard statistical corrections (Bonferroni for multiple comparisons, Cohen’s <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> for effect sizes) to make sure the results are not flukes; details in Appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We organize our experiments in two parts. First, we look inside the model: what the internal geometry looks like (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Tool identity fits in a small space ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) and which components are responsible (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Tracing the decision from input to output ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). Then we use this understanding to intervene: switching the model’s tool choice (Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Can we exploit this structure to control tool selection? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>), observing how the output adapts (Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 The model also rewrites its arguments ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>), and tracing when the structure develops across model scales (Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 When does this structure appear? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Tool identity fits in a small space</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by looking at what tool selection looks like inside the model. Each tool has a mean activation vector (2,560 numbers describing its position in the model’s internal space). With 15 tools, we have 15 such vectors. We want to know: how many independent directions do these 15 vectors actually span? If they spread out in all 2,560 dimensions, there is no simple structure. If they cluster into a handful of directions, the model has compressed tool identity into a small space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To measure this, we use principal component analysis (PCA): we find the directions along which the 15 tool vectors differ the most, rank them by how much of the total spread they explain, and count how many we need before we have captured most of it. Concretely, we center the 15 mean vectors, compute their singular value decomposition, and measure the cumulative fraction of variance explained by the top <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> components (details in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix A.7 PCA method details ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7</span></a>). The result is striking (Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Tool identity fits in a small space ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07990v1/fig3_pca_3d.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/155;" width="476" height="155" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Left: 3D PCA of 15-tool activations, one model per family. Right: cumulative variance converges to <math id="S4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math>90% at 10 PCs. Cross-source (39 tools) in Appendix <a href="#A18.SSx1" title="Cross-source tool subspace ‣ Appendix A.18 Cross-layer divergence and attention ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.18</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Across all models, the first 10 principal components capture about 91% of the variance. We define <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}</annotation></semantics></math> as the smallest number of components needed to reach 90%; this is consistently 9–11 regardless of model family or scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Why around 10? With 15 tools, the maximum possible is 14 dimensions (15 points can always fit in a 14-dimensional space). But some tools share semantic overlap (like <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">get_weather</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">get_news</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">get_stock_price</span> all retrieving information), which compresses certain dimensions together. The model only needs to keep tools separable enough for the final layer to tell them apart, so it uses fewer dimensions than the theoretical maximum. The exact number depends on the tool set; 10 out of 14 is a descriptive finding, not a theoretical prediction. One caveat: PCA looks for directions that are at right angles to each other, but neural networks can pack more concepts into the same space by using nearly-overlapping directions (a phenomenon called superposition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elhage et al., 2022</a>)</cite>). Our <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}</annotation></semantics></math> should be read as a rough measure of how many independent directions the model uses for tools, not as a precise count of internal features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To make sure this compression is meaningful and not just a geometric artifact, we run two sanity checks. First, we ask: if you just scatter 15 random points in a 2,560-dimensional space, how much variance would the top 10 components explain by chance? The answer is about 74%, far below the 93% we observe (this gap is enormous in statistical terms; <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z{=}96.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>96.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z{=}96.4</annotation></semantics></math>). Second, we check whether the compression is specific to tools by running a matched-prompt control: the same 15-tool synthetic prefix in the prompt, but the user query is a non-tool topic (Roman history, quantum physics, economics, and so on) instead of a tool-trigger query. The result is the opposite of the common “tools are more compressed” intuition: across four models we test (Gemma 3 4B/12B IT, Qwen 3 4B, Llama 3.1 8B), non-tool topic queries actually fit in slightly fewer dimensions than tool-trigger queries at K=15 (<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}</annotation></semantics></math> 7–9 for non-tool vs. 10–11 for tools; see Appendix <a href="#A26" title="Appendix A.26 K=15 matched-prompt control ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.26</span></a> for details). So the K=15 compression we report is not evidence of tool-specific structure: 15 distinct semantic topics under the same prompt prefix compress just as well, and a little tighter. The interventional evidence in later sections (steering, activation patching, within-topic probe) is what actually distinguishes tool identity from generic semantic structure; the PCA dimensionality count alone does not. Base models show comparable PCA spectra (top-10 variance 89–93%), so whatever the K=15 PCA captures, it is already in place after pretraining; we discuss what changes between base and IT in Section <a href="#S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2" title="The role of instruction tuning ‣ 4.5 When does this structure appear? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">One more control is worth running: is the 10-dimensional PCA spectrum picking up the tool names as tokens, or is it reading the descriptions? We rebuild the same 15-tool prompts with anonymous IDs (<span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tool_00</span> through <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tool_14</span>) in place of real names, keeping parameters and descriptions. Across four models (Gemma 3 4B IT and base, Qwen 3 4B, Llama 3.1 8B IT), PCA <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}</annotation></semantics></math> stays at 9–11 and the top-10 variance stays within 0.01–0.02 of the real-name version (Appendix <a href="#A27" title="Appendix A.27 Anonymous-name control ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.27</span></a>). So the low-dimensional PCA spectrum does not rely on the tool-name tokens themselves. This rules out the “the K=15 spectrum is just the tool-name token” reading; it does not rule out a description-topic reading, which we handle separately in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Discussion ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Another natural worry is that our subspace picks up the order in which tools appear in the list, not the tool identity itself. We shuffle the 15-tool list into five different orderings (identity, reverse, and three random permutations) and recompute each tool’s mean activation in every ordering. For Gemma 3 4B IT, the same tool’s mean direction stays remarkably consistent across orderings: the mean pairwise cosine similarity between the same tool’s representations under different orderings is 0.98 (range 0.95–0.99 across tools). The per-ordering PCA <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}</annotation></semantics></math> also stays at 9–11, matching the single-ordering number. An ANOVA-style decomposition of the variance across all (tool, ordering) means attributes roughly four times more variance to tool identity than to list position (16.6% vs 3.9%). The same pattern holds on Gemma 3 4B base, Qwen 3 4B, and Llama 3.1 8B IT, with cross-ordering cosine between 0.96 and 1.00 and the tool-to-position variance ratio between 4.3<math id="S4.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> and 4.9<math id="S4.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> (Appendix <a href="#A28" title="Appendix A.28 List-ordering control ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.28</span></a>). So the direction we use for each tool is not an artifact of where that tool sits in the prompt.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">A stronger test: does the compression hold as we add more tools? We scale up using real API definitions from ToolBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, sampling evenly across 49 domains (finance, health, sports, etc.) from a pool of 12,000+ APIs (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Tool identity fits in a small space ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
