<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>PCA scaling on Gemma 3 4B with real ToolBench APIs (stratified across 49 domains, with descriptions). <math id="S4.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}</annotation></semantics></math>: dimensions needed to explain 90% of variance. Compression improves with scale.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The trend continues from our 15-tool result (<math id="S4.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}{=}10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}{=}10</annotation></semantics></math>, Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Tool identity fits in a small space ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>): more tools require more dimensions, but the growth is much slower than random. At <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}200</annotation></semantics></math>, the model needs only 36 directions (18% of the theoretical max; random points would need 167).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Matched-prompt control.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The random-Gaussian baseline (167) is a weak comparison: any 200 mean activations sharing a long prompt prefix will compress better than iid noise, no matter what the query content is. We want a tighter control that holds everything fixed except the actual user query. So we keep the same prompt template, the same 200-tool prefix, and the same extraction position as before, and just swap out the user query for a non-tool topic (cities, biology, philosophy, music, sports, and similar, two queries per topic and mean over the two). Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Matched-prompt control. ‣ 4.1 Tool identity fits in a small space ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the resulting <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}</annotation></semantics></math> alongside the tool-means <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}</annotation></semantics></math> at the same model and layer. The picture is more nuanced than “tool means cluster much tighter than random”.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
