<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>Decomposing the steering vector against the target tool’s first-token unembedding direction, on 30 source<math id="S5.T9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>target pairs. Full: the original <math id="S5.T9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}_{b}-\bar{h}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{b}-\bar{h}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.T9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}0.7</annotation></semantics></math>. Par and Orth: natural parallel and orthogonal components of <math id="S5.T9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> (each at its own norm). Par<sup id="S5.T9.5" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.T9.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">⋆</span></sup> and Orth<sup id="S5.T9.6" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.T9.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">⋆</span></sup>: the same components rescaled to match the full vector’s L2 norm.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Two things follow. First, the signal that moves the model’s tool choice looks like it is carried by the parallel (unembedding-aligned) direction: steering along that direction alone, at full magnitude, is as effective as the original intervention. Second, the orthogonal remainder seems not to be the mechanism, even though it accounts for 98–99% of the vector’s norm; given the same magnitude, the orthogonal direction leaves the model’s tool choice almost untouched. So the cosine alignment we reported is not a spurious correlation, as far as our magnitude-matched test can tell: the aligned component is what actually does the steering.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">We are careful with the instruction-tuning story here. Both base and IT par<sup id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p15.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p15.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">⋆</span></sup> reach 100% at matched magnitude, so this experiment alone cannot pin the IT-vs-base gap on the parallel direction; at matched magnitude the direction simply wins out at the output, regardless of whether the underlying cosine is 0.13 or 0.29. The IT-vs-base gap that we see in full-vector steering (Gemma 3 4B: 97% IT vs 50% base at <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}0.7</annotation></semantics></math>) tracks the natural-magnitude alignment (IT cosine <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.26, base <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.13). The decomposition is consistent with this alignment being the quantity that matters, but a clean test would steer along par at <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p15.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">natural</em> (un-rescaled) magnitude on base vs IT, which is left for follow-up.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07990v1/fig_unembed_alignment.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/169;" width="476" height="169"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Steering vectors align with unembedding directions for the target tool. IT models show 2<math id="S5.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> higher alignment than base, and z-scores increase with scale.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Catching mistakes before they happen</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because the steering effect is linear, there is a simple way to tell when the model is about to pick the wrong tool. For each tool, we first collect its mean activation from 2 example queries: the average of the residual stream at the penultimate layer across those queries, which serves as that tool’s representative direction. Then, for a new query, we measure cosine similarity between the query’s activation and each tool’s mean. If the top two tools have very similar scores (a small gap), the model is torn between them and much more likely to make a mistake. If one tool is clearly ahead (a large gap), the model has made a clear decision and is usually right.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We test this on BFCL v3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, a function-calling benchmark with real user queries (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}500</annotation></semantics></math> leave-one-out queries per model). The picture splits cleanly between base (pretrained-only) and instruction-tuned models (Table <a href="#S5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Cosine baseline caveat. ‣ 5.2 Catching mistakes before they happen ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). On instruction-tuned models, the model’s own generation is already accurate, and cosine readout from the residual does not add anything beyond that: Gemma 3 4B IT generates the correct tool about 92% of the time and readout sits slightly below at 74%, with similar gaps for 12B IT (95% vs 66%) and Llama 3.1 8B IT (90% vs 53%). On base models the gap reverses. Gemma 3 4B base generates the correct tool on only 3% of queries, but cosine readout from the residual recovers 75%, a gain of around 72 percentage points. Similar gaps show up on Gemma 3 1B base (2% to 69%), Gemma 3 12B base (about 56 points), and Llama 3.1 8B base (10% to 82%). One reading of this gap is that pretraining already encodes tool identity well enough for a linear readout to recover it, but a base model does not yet know how to route that internal signal into structured output, and instruction tuning fills in that routing. We treat this as a suggestive interpretation and flag a competing one in the next paragraph.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cosine baseline caveat.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The readout depends on tool means being separable in cosine space, so we measured pairwise cosine between 200 mean activations under the matched-prompt protocol of Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Tool identity fits in a small space ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> (200-tool ToolBench prefix, last layer): tool-trigger queries against non-tool topic queries, on both IT and base models (Table <a href="#S5.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Cosine baseline caveat. ‣ 5.2 Catching mistakes before they happen ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). Two findings complicate the simple “pretraining forms a subspace, IT routes it to output” reading.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, on the Gemma IT family, tool and topic pair cosines are nearly identical (gap at most 0.036 on 12B, 0.003 on 4B), so the readout has very little room to discriminate among tools regardless of how well the underlying tool signal is formed. That gives an alternative reading for why IT readout sits below IT generation on Gemma; the routing might not have moved at all, the cosine geometry has just compressed. On Qwen 3 4B and Llama 3.1 8B the picture flips: tool means actually spread <em id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">wider</em> than non-tool topic means (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{=}-0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{=}-0.15</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.07" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.07</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.07</annotation></semantics></math>), so the cosine-compression story does not apply, and yet Llama 8B IT readout still sits below its generation. The cosine-compression explanation cannot account for the IT-vs-base pattern in general.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, on the base models we check, the geometry is much tighter than on IT. Gemma 3 4B-pt clusters all activations at cos <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.999 (std 0.0007 across 200 means), and 12B-pt similarly at cos <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.998 (std 0.001). The base-model cosine readout that recovers 69–82% on BFCL therefore works through argmax differences in the third or fourth decimal place of cosine, not through a geometrically separable subspace. So when we say “the tool subspace is already there in base models”, we mean “a linear classifier can pick out the right tool from the residual”, not “the means sit in clearly distinct directions”. We treat the IT-vs-base interpretation in the previous paragraph as suggestive rather than a clean causal story.</p>
</div>
