<td id="S5.T11.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S5.T11.2.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Instruction-tuned models</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T11.2.8" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">
<td id="S5.T11.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T11.2.8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">Gemma 3 4B IT</span></td>
<td id="S5.T11.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T11.2.8.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">92</span></td>
<td id="S5.T11.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T11.2.8.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">74</span></td>
<td id="S5.T11.2.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T11.m3" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;" mathbackground="#EDEDED">−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.T11.2.8.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">17</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T11.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T11.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Gemma 3 12B IT</td>
<td id="S5.T11.2.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">95</td>
<td id="S5.T11.2.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">66</td>
<td id="S5.T11.2.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>30</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T11.2.10" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">
<td id="S5.T11.2.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T11.2.10.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">Llama 3.1 8B IT</span></td>
<td id="S5.T11.2.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T11.2.10.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">90</span></td>
<td id="S5.T11.2.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T11.2.10.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">53</span></td>
<td id="S5.T11.2.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T11.m5" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;" mathbackground="#EDEDED">−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.T11.2.10.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EDEDED;">37</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 11: </span>BFCL v3 tool-name accuracy: greedy generation vs. cosine readout of the penultimate-layer residual against per-tool means (max_new_tokens=200, thinking tags stripped). Base models encode the tool before they can emit it; instruction tuning closes that output-side gap.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Reading off Table <a href="#S5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Cosine baseline caveat. ‣ 5.2 Catching mistakes before they happen ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, the base/IT split is large and consistent. The exact base-generation number is parser-sensitive: a strict parser (the one we use for Table <a href="#S5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Cosine baseline caveat. ‣ 5.2 Catching mistakes before they happen ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) gives Gemma 3 4B base 3%, while a substring-matching parser on the same outputs gives 26% on the same base model. The readout number is stable across parsers (74–83% on Gemma 3 4B base depending on the matching rule), so what is parser-sensitive is the size of the gap, not its sign or its order of magnitude. A separate use of the same cosine geometry is to predict errors: the gap between the top two tools in activation space tracks when the model is about to misroute. On Gemma 3 12B, the quartile with the smallest gap has a 14% error rate while the quartile with the largest gap has 0%, and flagging below-median gaps catches 92% of errors. On Gemma 3 27B, the same pattern holds: the lowest-confidence quartile has a 17% error rate vs. 1% for the highest, a 21<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> concentration ratio. This pattern is weaker on smaller models and on Llama, suggesting it depends on how cleanly each model separates its tools internally.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tool selection in language models turns out to be linearly readable and intervenable, at least in the single-turn, fixed-menu setting we focus on. Across three model families and scales from 270M to 27B, adding a mean-difference vector to the residual stream switches among 15 tools at 77–100% accuracy (93–100% on 4B+ instruction-tuned models, with descriptions offering at most a small boost), and the JSON output that follows matches the new tool’s schema through ordinary autoregressive generation. The steering effect is direction-specific: random vectors at the same norm give 0% switch, while a unit vector along the unembedding-aligned direction at matched magnitude reaches 93–100% on its own. Activation patching localizes the decision to a few specific attention heads (L17 H0/H1 in Gemma 3 4B), and a within-topic probe on <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-bench airline reaches top-1 61–89% even when all 14 tools share the same domain. We are careful with the geometric framing: PCA dimensionality at K=15 and K=200 does not survive matched-prompt controls (non-tool topic queries with the same prompt prefix compress equally well, sometimes more), so we read the result as “tool identity has a linear, intervenable representation” rather than “tool identity occupies a uniquely small subspace”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also validate the linear encoding on BFCL v3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. On base models, reading tool identity from the residual stream via cosine similarity recovers 69–82% tool-name accuracy even though the base model’s own generation lands between 2 and 10%, a gap of roughly 56–72 percentage points (Table <a href="#S5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Cosine baseline caveat. ‣ 5.2 Catching mistakes before they happen ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). On instruction-tuned models, the model’s generation already reaches 90–95%, and cosine readout falls below that. The simplest reading is that pretraining encodes tool identity well enough for a linear classifier to recover it, and instruction tuning supplies the routing into structured output. We caveat that on the Gemma family the IT cosine geometry is itself compressed (tool and topic pair cosines within 0.04 of each other) and the base geometry is even tighter (cos <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.998 with std <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.001), so the readout works through argmax over very small differences rather than over a geometrically separable subspace; the readout-vs-generation gap is therefore suggestive of pretraining encoding tool identity, not a clean causal proof of where it sits.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are clear limits to what we have shown, and being upfront about them matters for anyone who might want to build on this work. The linear structure only appears above 270M parameters; sub-1B models do not have exploitable structure (Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 When does this structure appear? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>). Steering breaks down when tools are near-synonymous (0% on 10 similar Sports APIs; Appendix), which is really a limit of the model itself: if the model cannot distinguish two tools, neither can we. Tool identity is linear but argument content is not: steering can change <em id="Sx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which</em> tool gets called but not <em id="Sx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">what values</em> get passed to it (Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Can we exploit this structure to control tool selection? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>). Steered outputs match the target schema (Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.4 The model also rewrites its arguments ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) but sometimes fabricate arguments (like “Pizza Palace” for a restaurant name). A real API server would likely reject such inputs. Our evaluation measures whether the model picks the right tool and produces the right JSON structure, not whether the arguments would actually work in production. Steering should be viewed as an analytical and routing tool (as in cosine select), not as a standalone method for end-to-end API execution; measuring actual task completion is the natural next step. Multi-turn behaviour is mixed. On a synthetic two-turn setup with a fixed menu, applying single-turn vectors at the second turn gives 100% at 4B+. On a realistic agent benchmark (<math id="Sx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-bench airline), comparing against fresh, non-crashing baselines gives a much smaller and model-dependent effect: the matched-vs-baseline delta ranges from <math id="Sx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>30 to <math id="Sx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>10 percentage points across five 4B–14B models (Gemma 3 4B / 12B, Qwen 3 4B / 8B / 14B), with Gemma 12B L47 and Qwen 8B showing the largest drops. We read this as the linear steering signal being reliable in single-turn, fixed-menu settings but competing for residual-stream capacity, or interacting badly with accumulated tool outputs, once the conversation carries substantial history. Longer multi-turn chains beyond this remain untested, and closing this gap is the main practical follow-up. There is also a practical limit on prompt length. When tool definitions include descriptions, the prompt grows to  16K tokens at <math id="Sx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>750</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}750</annotation></semantics></math>, and at that point Gemma 3 4B’s per-tool representations start to blur together (<math id="Sx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}</annotation></semantics></math> drops from 57 to 27). Without descriptions, the same model handles <math id="Sx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}1500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}1500</annotation></semantics></math> tools smoothly (<math id="Sx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}</annotation></semantics></math> grows to 116 with no collapse), because the prompt stays shorter ( 8K tokens). The bottleneck is not the number of tools but the total prompt length. We see the same pattern with tool descriptions in steering (Appendix <a href="#A23" title="Appendix A.23 Does adding tool descriptions change the results? ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.23</span></a>): instruction-tuned models at 4B+ already reach 93–100% without descriptions and gain at most a few points from them, while smaller models (Qwen 0.6B) and base models get <em id="Sx1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">worse</em>, because the longer prompt overwhelms their ability to focus on the right parts. This limit also depends on architecture: at <math id="Sx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}500</annotation></semantics></math> with descriptions, 8-head models (Gemma 3 4B) need 57 dimensions, 16-head models (Gemma 3 12B) need 129, and 32-head models (Qwen) need 147–151 (Appendix <a href="#A30.T33" title="Table 33 ‣ Cross-model comparison: attention heads predict subspace size. ‣ Appendix A.30 PCA scaling with ToolBench APIs ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>). More heads and more parameters both buy stability: at <math id="Sx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>750</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}750</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="Sx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}16</annotation></semantics></math>K tokens), Gemma 3 4B’s <math id="Sx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}</annotation></semantics></math> drops to 27 and does not recover at <math id="Sx1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}1000/1500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1500</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}1000/1500</annotation></semantics></math>, but Gemma 3 12B and 27B dip only briefly (<math id="Sx1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="131{\to}130" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>131</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>130</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">131{\to}130</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="Sx1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}500{\to}1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}500{\to}1000</annotation></semantics></math> for 27B) and fully recover their compression (Figure <a href="#A8.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix A.8 PCA dimensionality vs. tool count ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). Scale alone extends the effective context: 32-head models show no collapse even at <math id="Sx1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>750</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}750</annotation></semantics></math>, and larger Gemma models (12B/27B) tolerate at least <math id="Sx1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}1000</annotation></semantics></math> before prompt length outgrows the context budget. And all three families we test (Gemma 3, Qwen 3 / 2.5, Llama 3.1) are dense transformers; we do not know if this extends to mixture-of-experts or state-space models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Reproducibility Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All models are publicly available on HuggingFace (Table <a href="#A21.T23" title="Table 23 ‣ Appendix A.21 Models and resources ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>). SAEs are from Gemma Scope 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lieberum et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and NeuronPedia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin, 2024</a>)</cite>. We use TransformerLens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nanda and Bloom, 2022</a>)</cite> via SAELens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bloom et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for activation caching and steering. Steering vectors are computed from 2–3 queries per tool with no training. For the 27B model, we verified results using HuggingFace <span id="Sx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">transformers</span> with <span id="Sx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">device_map=auto</span>. Code and results will be released upon acceptance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethics Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our steering method redirects a model’s tool selection at inference time, which is dual-use: an operator with white-box access to an open-weight model could in principle silently reroute consequential tool calls without leaving an output-level trace. We see the net effect of this work as defender-side. The same per-tool mean activations that enable steering also flag likely-wrong calls (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Discussion ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), and the mechanistic visibility lets evaluators audit which components carry tool selection before trusting an agent with consequential actions. All models we study are publicly available on HuggingFace, so the work does not widen access to any system; output-level schema validation and allowlisting provide an independent defense against redirects, whether malicious or accidental.</p>
</div>
</section>
