<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 29: </span>List-ordering control. <span id="A28.T29.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">cos</span>: mean pairwise cosine similarity of the same tool’s mean activation across five orderings. <math id="A28.T29.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{90}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>90</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{90}</annotation></semantics></math>: the smallest number of PCA components to reach 90% variance (range across the five per-ordering runs). <span id="A28.T29.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tool</span> / <span id="A28.T29.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pos</span>: the fraction of total variance attributed to tool identity and to list position under an additive tool-plus-position decomposition. <span id="A28.T29.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Ratio</span>: tool variance divided by position variance. Across all five models, the same tool sits in essentially the same direction regardless of where it appears in the list, and tool identity accounts for roughly four to five times more variance than position.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A28.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A28.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The baseline tool-pick rate (no steering) under these orderings stays close to 100% on the instruction-tuned 4B+ models at every list position, with occasional dips at the last slot when a longer tool name happens to land there. On Gemma 3 1B IT, which has lower baseline accuracy overall, the dips are more scattered and not concentrated at any one position, which is consistent with a generally weaker tool-calling signal rather than a systematic primacy or recency bias.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A29" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A.29 </span>Within-topic probe details</h2>

<div id="A29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A29.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix backs up the fifth piece of evidence against the topic-injection story in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Discussion ‣ Tool Calling is Linearly Readable and Steerable in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Holding the topic roughly constant, we ask whether the linear signal can still tell tools apart.</p>
</div>
<section id="A29.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="A29.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-bench airline (same domain, different actions).</h4>

<div id="A29.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A29.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All 14 airline tools share one topic but differ in action (book, cancel, update flights, update passengers, update baggages, search direct flight, search onestop flight, get user details, get reservation details, transfer to human agent, and so on). For each model, we take real multi-turn trajectories from the matched-context run, collect the residual stream at the position where the model is about to emit a tool name, and train a multinomial logistic-regression probe (80/20 stratified split, <math id="A29.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> regularization <math id="A29.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>, StandardScaler features). We only keep tools with at least 10 calls in the trajectory pool per model, so <math id="A29.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> varies between 5 and 9.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A29.T30" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A29.T30.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A29.T30.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A29.T30.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="A29.T30.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Model</span></th>
